It is pretty obvious that triangles DEF and BCF share altitude. Also, since BD is a median,

[asy]
size(6cm);defaultpen(fontsize(11));
pair b =(0,0);pair c = (10, 0);pair a=(4, 6);
pair d= midpoint(b--c);
pair e = a + (1/3) * (c - a);
pair f = d + 0.25 * (a - d);
draw(a--b--c--cycle--d--e--f);

label("$A$", a, N);
label("$B$", b, SW);
label("$C$", c, SE);
label("$D$", d, S);
label("$E$", e, NE);
label("$F$", f, SW);

draw(subpath(a..b..d, 0.35 .. 1));
draw(subpath(e..d..f, 0.55 .. 0.85));
draw(subpath(a..b..c, 0.4 .. 1));

label("D", (2.5,2), NW);
label("E", (6.5, 1), NE);
label("F", (0.75, 2.5), SW);[/asy]

so

\begin{align*}\frac{area \triangle DEF }{area \triangle DBC} &= \frac{DF}{DB}\\ &=\frac{1}{4}.\end{align*}

Since $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$,  $area \triangle DBC = area \triangle ADB =\frac{1}{2}area \triangle ABC$. Thus,

\begin{align*}area \triangle DEF &= \frac{DF}{DB} area \triangle DBC\\ &= \frac{1}{4}\cdot \frac{1}{2}area \triangle ABC\\ &=\frac{1}{8} area \triangle ABC.\end{align*}



The main observation to make from this is that triangles $DEF$ and $ABC$ share a common angle at $A$, and since $F$ is $\frac{3}{4}$ of the way from $D$ to $B$,

\begin{align*}\frac{area \triangle DEF }{area \triangle ABC} &= \frac{DF}{DB}\\ &=\frac{3}{4}.\end{align*}

Thus,

\begin{align*}area \triangle DEF &= \frac{3}{4} area \triangle ABC\\ \frac{1}{8}area \triangle ABC &= 17\\ area \triangle ABC &= 136.\end{align*}

</answer>

